#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152905} :activity/id #uuid "3425e60e-6be4-5837-af4c-757f20dfe1bd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049833 :topic/id #uuid "175206ba-6044-598d-974f-886a69e03c69" :topic/math-academy-id 2622} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153455 :tutorial/content "You may have noticed that the congruence of integers modulo $n$ satisfies the following important properties:\n\n- The *reflexive* property. Every integer is congruent to itself:\n$a \\equiv a \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- The *symmetric* property. If $a$ is congruent to $b$, then $b$ is congruent to $a{:}$\n$a \\equiv b \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b \\equiv a \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- The *transitive* property. If $a$ is congruent to $b$, and $b$ is congruent to $c$, then $a$ is congruent to $c{:}$\n$a \\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad b \\equiv c \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\equiv c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\nGenerally, if a relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it's called an **equivalence relation**. The congruence of integers modulo $n$ is an example of an equivalence relation.\n\nThe symbol \"$\\sim$\" is often used to represent an equivalence relation. So, if $R$ is an equivalence relation, instead of $(x,y) \\in R$ or $x \\: R \\: y$, one can simply write $x \\sim y$.\n\nSome other important equivalence relations include the following:\n\n- Equality ($=$) over $\\mathbb R$\n- Parity over $\\mathbb Z$\n- Triangle similarity over the (infinite) set containing all possible triangles\n\nIn particular, you may recall that when two triangles $P$ and $Q$ are similar, we write $P\\sim Q$. Now you know why we use this notation!\n\nEquivalence relations are important because they encode our natural intuitions (namely, reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity) describing what it means for two objects to be, in some sense, equal, equivalent, or \"the same.\"\n\nTo understand this concretely, suppose we have a bag containing many colored balls, and we sort them into groups according to their color. Then, the following properties must be true:\n\n- Each ball is the same color as itself. Therefore, each ball satisfies the reflexive property.\n- If ball $x$ is the same color as ball $y$, then ball $y$ is the same color as ball $x$. Therefore, the balls within a given group satisfy the symmetric property.\n- If ball $x$ is the same color as ball $y$, and ball $y$ is the same color as ball $z$, then ball $x$ must be the same color as ball $z$. Therefore, the balls within a given group satisfy the transitive property.\n\nSince the relation \"is the same color as\" over the set of balls is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it's an equivalence relation." :tutorial/id #uuid "e46a3d30-f990-5638-880b-dc4706f1f270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9026 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2acce04f-6176-5008-b722-7681e49061c7" :step/math-academy-id 137138 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151870 :question/id #uuid "de6949c6-850c-511e-8bea-bb246972cda4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b70dd396-b01a-51e6-a52c-8ff729f48c45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050659}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152505}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Standard Relation Is an Equivalence"} :step/id #uuid "9f603f02-8c7b-5bb5-bdda-b292cf6d3ee4" :step/math-academy-id 137140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151845 :question/id #uuid "6b0a0a3a-7079-5798-b2b3-14f7165ade81" :question/math-academy-id "e-14599"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152503}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "8fa517f6-46e0-5d86-b1b8-4442b8fc0ce3" :step/math-academy-id 137143 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151844 :question/id #uuid "de1fa48e-853a-5aa4-ab32-f23540ef1dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14598"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152726}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"} :step/id #uuid "bf9df65c-9f0b-5ab3-b36e-be8bdf669cfd" :step/math-academy-id 137146}] :activity/title "Equivalence Relations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152329}]}
